  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 410 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nAbsolutely gorgeous late summer weather out there this afternoon\nand evening. Get out and enjoy it if you can. Quiet conditions\nlook to persist tonight and through most, if not all, of the\ndaylight hours Monday. Highs should climb 3-5 degrees most\nlocation for Monday given the quieter/sunnier start to the day,\nbut still seasonable in the 83-88 degree range. \n\nNext chance for storms will come from convection that develops\nalong a cold front over north central Nebraska late Monday \nafternoon, then moves SE during the evening and overnight. \nExpect a general evolution - in terms of intensity - fairly \nsimilar to recent weeks in that the strongest activity will be \nprior to 9-10PM, then gradual weakening thereafter. This would \nplace the highest severe chances just NW of the local area, \nright where SPC has a Slight Risk (level 2 of 5). What does make\nit into the area would probably be in the form of one or two \nsmall clusters and pose primarily a risk for 50-60 MPH gusts.\n\nThe aforementioned front looks to drape somewhere across the\narea on Tuesday - with the ultimate location being somewhat\nreliant upon how strong/widespread Monday night's activity is.\nNorthern Kansas is most likely to remain storm/outflow free the\nlongest, so these locations could get quite warm by Tuesday\nafternoon with forecast highs in the mid to upper 90s. Add in a\nlittle bit of humidity (not as much as further E/NE, though) and\nheat indices could sneak into the lower 100s. The weather\nheadline for Tuesday, however, will be chances for at least\nscattered storms to develop over the area during the late\nafternoon and evening. Pooling moisture and insolation ahead of\nthe composite outflow/front should support moderate to strong\ninstability with MLCAPE values in excess of 2-3K J/kg. Forecast\nsoundings from various guidance at 21Z Tue also show seasonably\nstrong deep layer shear of 30-40kt amidst relatively straight\nhodographs above 1-2km. This should support initial splitting\nsupercells with both large hail and wind threat...then gradual\nupscale growth into one or two clusters and transition to\nprimarily a damaging wind threat for the late evening into \nearly overnight hours. Agree with the latest SPC outlook showing\na broad Slight risk category across the area - including the \nrecent inclusion of a Cig 1 delineation.\n\nExpect cooler temperatures behind the front Wednesday-Thursday,\nand may see another couple rounds of showers and storms, most\nfavored during the evening and overnight hours. Confidence on\ndetails, though, is quite low.\n\nPattern may quiet down some heading into next weekend and warm\nback to, or even above, seasonable levels for temperatures.",
